With terminals nowadays, they tend to all claim to be xterm, regardless
of whether they're derived from xterm or not. (screen is a notable
exception, incidentaly. So is urxvt nowadays, but it used to claim to be
xterm in the distant past.) Thus, xterm tends to be a safe guess.

Actually, terminal emulators are so homogeneous nowadays that NetHack 4
uses a new rendering library (libuncursed) that completely ignores $TERM
and what the terminal claims about its capabilities, and just sends the
most portable terminal control codes it can (after a quick check for
Unicode support). This has the advantage that it makes watching games
and replaying ttyrecs more portable, and several other advantages too,
but the disadvantage that it won't work on an old-fashioned physical
VT100 (because it doesn't insert delays where the VT100 needs them).
Possibly the biggest advantage is darkgray that works portably across
terminals (apart from the very few that can't render it at all); the
usual darkgray patch for 3.4.3 renders it as black on many terminals,
which is a problem.

